data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_01722f5bf721c5ab006a51beeb78f881909c0d6e4ba7572688","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":126,"text":"“**Foundry Agent Service**” most commonly refers to **Microsoft Foundry Agent Service** (part of Microsoft Foundry on Azure): a **managed platform for building, deploying, and scaling AI agents**. It provides a runtime plus a single API surface so your agents can use supported models and call tools (search, files, code execution, etc.) to complete multi-step tasks. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\n## What it is (in practical terms)\n- **Agents** are AI apps that don’t just chat—they can **reason, decide, and take actions** by calling tools and working over multiple steps. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))  \n- Foundry Agent Service gives you:\n  - A **Responses API** as a single entry point to access models + tools\n  - An **Agent Runtime** that hosts and scales agents and manages conversations/tool calls\n  - **Built-in tools** (web search, file search, code interpreter, memory, MCP servers, and custom functions)\n  - **Observability** (tracing/metrics) and **identity/security** integration. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\n## Agent types you can run\nMicrosoft Foundry Agent Service supports (at a high level) two main patterns:\n- **Prompt agents**: define behavior primarily via instructions/prompts; Foundry runs it for you without you managing app compute. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))  \n- **Hosted agents**: you bring agent code (including popular agent frameworks), package it (commonly as a container), and Foundry runs it behind a managed endpoint with scaling/identity/observability. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\n## Tools (what agents can “do”)\nTools are optional capabilities you attach so the model can pick them when useful—generally in two buckets:\n- **Knowledge tools** (ground the agent in data): e.g., web/Bing search, enterprise search, file search, data platforms. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/agents/how-to/tools-classic/overview))  \n- **Action tools** (do work): e.g., workflow automation, calling APIs via OpenAPI, MCP tool connections, function calling, etc. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/agents/how-to/tools-classic/overview))  \n\n## “Classic” vs new Agents Service (important)\nMicrosoft’s documentation notes that **Foundry (classic) agents are deprecated and scheduled to be retired on March 31, 2027**, and Microsoft recommends using the **new agents** experience/API going forward. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/agents/how-to/tools-classic/overview))\n\n## Quick clarification\nAre you asking about **Microsoft Foundry Agent Service (Azure)** (docs above), or **Palantir Foundry** (a different “Foundry” product entirely)? If you tell me which one—and whether you’re trying to *build an agent* or just understand features/pricing—I’ll tailor the explanation."}

